            0.364,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 7"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.186,
            0.09,
            0.849,
            0.172
          ],
          "content": "the remaining part of the land to her daughter Krishna and $\\frac { 1 } { 3 }$ of it to her son Bora. After giving them their shares, she keeps the remaining land for herself."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.175,
            0.721,
            0.196
          ],
          "content": "(a) What part of the original land did Krishna get?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.199,
            0.69,
            0.22
          ],
          "content": "(b) What part of the original land did Bora get?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.223,
            0.823,
            0.244
          ],
          "content": "(c) What part of the original land did Somu keep for herself?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.251,
            0.701,
            0.286
          ],
          "content": "3. Find the area of a rectangle of sides 3 $\\frac { 3 } { 4 }$ ft and 9 $\\frac{3}{5}$ ft."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.29,
            0.849,
            0.401
          ],
          "content": "4. Tsewang plants four saplings in a row in his garden. The distance between two saplings is $\\frac { 3 } { 4 }$ m. Find the distance between the first and last sapling. [**Hint:** Draw a rough diagram with four saplings with distance between two saplings as $\\frac { 3 } { 4 }$ m]"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.402,
            0.664,
            0.439
          ],
          "content": "5. Which is heavier: $\\frac { 1 2 } { 1 5 }$ of 500 grams or $\\frac{3}{20}$ of 4 kg?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.447,
            0.847,
            0.492
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Is the Product Always Greater than the Numbers Multiplied?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.5,
            0.848,
            0.556
          ],
          "content": "Since, we know that when a number is multiplied by 1, the product remains unchanged, we will look at multiplying pairs of numbers where neither of them is 1."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.556,
            0.847,
            0.595
          ],
          "content": "When we multiply two counting numbers greater than 1, say 3 and 5, the product is greater than both the numbers being multiplied."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.446,
            0.603,
            0.55,
            0.623
          ],
          "content": "3 \\times 5 = 15",
          "image_source": "images/page_11_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.627,
            0.604,
            0.648
          ],
          "content": "The product, 15, is more than both 3 and 5."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.65,
            0.632,
            0.684
          ],
          "content": "But what happens when we multiply $\\frac { 1 } { 4 }$ and 8?"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.454,
            0.689,
            0.543,
            0.723
          ],
          "content": "\\frac { 1 } { 4 } \\times 8 = 2",
          "image_source": "images/page_11_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.725,
            0.846,
            0.777
          ],
          "content": "In the above multiplication the product, 2, is greater than $\\frac{1}{4},$ but less than 8."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.79,
            0.6,
            0.825
          ],
          "content": "What happens when we multiply $\\frac { 3 } { 4 }$ and $\\frac{2}{5}$ ?"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.449,
            0.84,
            0.55,
            0.877
          ],
          "content": "{ \\frac { 3 } { 4 } } \\times { \\frac { 2 } { 5 } } = { \\frac { 6 } { 2 0 } }",
          "image_source": "images/page_11_equation_17.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.88,
            0.846,
            0.917
          ],
          "content": "Let us compare this product $\\frac { 6 } { 2 0 }$ with the numbers $\\frac { 3 } { 4 }$ and ${ \\frac { 2 } { 5 } } .$ For this,"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.155,
            0.938,
            0.194,
            0.955
          ],
          "content": "184"
        },
